  "explanation": {
    "markdown": "## What is reported speech or indirect speech?\n\nWhen we tell people **what another person said or thought**, we often use **reported speech** or indirect speech. To do that, we need to change verb tenses (present, past, etc.) and pronouns (**I, you, my, your, etc**.) if the time and speaker are different.\n\n-   Sally: *‘**I** **don’t have** time.’* ⇒ *Sally said that **she didn’t have** time.* \n-   Peter: *‘**I am** tired .’* ⇒ *He said that **he was** tired.* \n\n## Omission of **that**\n\nWe often leave out **that** after reporting verbs like **say, think**, **etc.** \n\n-   *She **said she** was late.* (=*She said that she was late.*)\n-   *I **thought I** would get the job.*  \n\n## **Say** or **tell**?\n\nThe most common verbs we use in reported speech are [**say** and **tell**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/shorts/say-vs-tell-grammar-shorts/). We must pay attention here. We say **tell** **somebody** **something** and **say something (to somebody)**.\n\n-   *They **said me** (that) they would help me. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/reported-speech-indirect-speech/img-1.png)* \n-   *They **told me** (that) they would help me.* ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/reported-speech-indirect-speech/img-2.png)\n-   *He **told** (that) he didn’t have a car. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/reported-speech-indirect-speech/img-3.png)* \n-   *He **said** (that) he didn’t have a car.* ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/reported-speech-indirect-speech/img-4.png)\n\n## Tense changes in reported speech\n\nWhen a person said something **in the past**, and **now** we tell somebody what that person said, the time is different, and for this reason, the verb tenses change. Look at a summary of these changes.  \n  \n![Direct speech vs reported speech/indirect speech](/uploads/grammar/a2/reported-speech-indirect-speech/img-5.png)\n\n## Pronoun changes in reported speech\n\nIn reported or indirect speech, we must also pay attention to the use of [pronouns](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a2/subject-pronouns-object-pronouns-possessive-pronouns-possessive-adjectives/). When a person tells us something, he or she uses the first person (**I, me, my, we, us, our**) to talk about himself or herself and the second person (**you, your**) to talk about us, the person listening. But when we tell someone else what that person said, we are going to use the third person (**he, she, his, her, etc.**) to talk about the speaker and the first person (**I, me, my**) to talk about ourselves, the listener.\n\n-   *‘**I** will help **you**.’* ⇒ *He said that **he** would help **me**.* \n-   *‘That’s **my** pen.’* ⇒ *She said that it was **her** pen.* \n-   *‘**I** need **your** help.’* ⇒ *She said that **she** needed **my** help.*",
